sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Mephibosheth","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":3,"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.4","rawReference":"2Sam.4.4"},{"anchorIndex":7,"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.1-12","rawReference":"2Sam.4.1-12"}],"ruleId":"Mephibosheth::Mephibosheth::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"Mephibosheth"},"text":"And Jonathan son of Saul had a son who was lame in his feet. He was five years old when the report about Saul and Jonathan came from Jezreel. His nurse picked him up and fled, but as she was hurrying to escape, he fell and became lame. His name was Mephibosheth.","verse":4}],"id":"Mephibosheth","label":"Mephibosheth","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Mephibosheth::Mephibosheth::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Original complete headword and source opening identify a named person, people, or place. Promotion requires a capitalized whole name and the original article explicitly citing this same BSB verse. No arbitrary qualifier stripping or book-title inference. This citation-constrained rule was reviewed once globally; unmatched contexts remain excluded.","sourceHeadwordId":"Mephibosheth","term":"Mephibosheth"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Mephibosheth","sourceTitle":"Mephibosheth"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Saul","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul"},"text":"Now when Ish-bosheth son of Saul heard that Abner had died in Hebron, he lost courage, and all Israel was dismayed.","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Saul","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul"},"text":"Saul’s son had two men who were leaders of raiding parties. One was named Baanah and the other Rechab; they were sons of Rimmon the Beerothite of the tribe of Benjamin—Beeroth is considered part of Benjamin,","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Saul","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul"},"text":"And Jonathan son of Saul had a son who was lame in his feet. He was five years old when the report about Saul and Jonathan came from Jezreel. His nurse picked him up and fled, but as she was hurrying to escape, he fell and became lame. His name was Mephibosheth.","verse":4},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Saul","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul"},"text":"They brought the head of Ish-bosheth to David at Hebron and said to the king, “Here is the head of Ish-bosheth son of Saul, your enemy who sought your life. Today the LORD has granted vengeance to my lord the king against Saul and his offspring.”","verse":8},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Saul","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul"},"text":"when someone told me, ‘Look, Saul is dead,’ and thought he was a bearer of good news, I seized him and put him to death at Ziklag. That was his reward for his news!","verse":10}],"id":"Saul","label":"Saul","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Saul::Saul::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Saul","term":"Saul"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Saul","sourceTitle":"Saul"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.1","r":"2Sam.4.1","rawR":"2Sam.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.1","value":"2Sam.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["all Israel became paralyzed with fear:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Abner’s true power was evident in Ishbosheth’s reaction to his death (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.2.9-11","r":"Josh.2.9-11","rawR":"Josh.2.9-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.2.9-11","value":"Josh.2.9-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 2:9-11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.5.1","r":"Josh.5.1","rawR":"Josh.5.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.5.1","value":"Josh.5.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1"},"). Ishbosheth’s timidity bred the same among his followers."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.1","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"119c587d84cef3d79fb673e43ea61ebda12d6c41f8ad42eee7a00c8de8e809fd","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3721,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.2-3","r":"2Sam.4.2-3","rawR":"2Sam.4.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.2-3","value":"2Sam.4.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:2-3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Beeroth"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was located in Benjamin near Gibeon. Probably because of Saul’s persecution of the non-Israelite residents in that region (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.21.1-9","r":"2Sam.21.1-9","rawR":"2Sam.21.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.21.1-9","value":"2Sam.21.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:1-9"},"), the citizens of ",{"c":["Beeroth"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," had ",{"c":["fled to Gittaim"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," the location of which is unknown."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.2-3","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.2-3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2eecfccf151487ef4397ae5b2d52dcfadcdc91e07e8ffda392f98a8306a10f9d","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.2-3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3722,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.2-3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.2-3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.4","r":"2Sam.4.4","rawR":"2Sam.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.4","value":"2Sam.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This parenthetical statement shows that, with the death of Ishbosheth (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.5-7","r":"2Sam.4.5-7","rawR":"2Sam.4.5-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.5-7","value":"2Sam.4.5-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5-7"},"), Saul’s dynasty was unable to continue—the only other heir was a young child who was crippled (see also ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.9.1-13","r":"2Sam.9.1-13","rawR":"2Sam.9.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.9.1-13","value":"2Sam.9.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:1-13"},"). • ",{"c":["Mephibosheth"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is a nickname meaning “from the mouth of shame,” possibly referring to his physical condition. No crippled animal could ever be offered to God, and no disabled priest was allowed to stand before God with the people’s offerings (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.21.19","r":"Lev.21.19","rawR":"Lev.21.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.21.19","value":"Lev.21.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 21:19"},"). Likewise, Mephibosheth’s condition probably disqualified him from the crown. His name change from Merib-baal (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.9.40","r":"1Chr.9.40","rawR":"1Chr.9.40","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.9.40","value":"1Chr.9.40"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 9:40"},") also removed association with Baal (see ",{"href":"?item=IISam.2.8_StudyNote_Filament","id":"IISam.2.8_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"IISam.2.8_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 2 Sam 2:8"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.4","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ab9294e0a4f1718143dd76e393726505df9282d1f5ed741ae24fd51e4fbc803f","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3723,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.6","r":"2Sam.4.6","rawR":"2Sam.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.6","value":"2Sam.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Recab"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["Baanah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," believed they would gain David’s favor by committing this treacherous act."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.6","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"7a7538d35dbee9d97b483f194b2c3da53c02ed48ea8f713db502d3801469686e","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3724,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.8","r":"2Sam.4.8","rawR":"2Sam.4.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.8","value":"2Sam.4.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["your enemy Saul:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," As far as we know, David never referred to Saul as an enemy. • ",{"c":["the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"},{"c":[" has given . . . revenge:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Wrongdoers often presume upon God’s favor to justify political ambition. However, David would not reward treachery."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.8","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"03c6042ca616d52bfd1fdbd72be85a6e2495f7a30233fc84e3232c2e60f51d79","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3725,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.9-11","r":"2Sam.4.9-11","rawR":"2Sam.4.9-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.9-11","value":"2Sam.4.9-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:9-11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Someone once told me:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Cp. ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.1.1-16","r":"2Sam.1.1-16","rawR":"2Sam.1.1-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.1.1-16","value":"2Sam.1.1-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:1-16"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.9-11","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.9-11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c11ec5d71713eb372ac6158ae8e36d1968bf2a2193baf7fb4d7f10c7fd7cbca6","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.9-11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3726,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.9-11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.9-11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.12","r":"2Sam.4.12","rawR":"2Sam.4.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.12","value":"2Sam.4.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["cut off their hands and feet:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Displaying the dismembered bodies of Ishbosheth’s killers shamed them by denying them proper burial (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.8-13","r":"1Sam.31.8-13","rawR":"1Sam.31.8-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.8-13","value":"1Sam.31.8-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 31:8-13"},"); it was also a public testimony of David’s innocence in Ishbosheth’s death."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IISam.4.12","refs":[{"book":"2SA","endChapter":4,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IISam.4.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2f56e267b5089cf1140d02758f61003b3bcb0bb620bb7ca9c97beecd60df5c7c","sourceItemName":"IISam.4.12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3727,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2SA","canonicalOSIS":"2Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Sam.4.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IISam.4.12","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}